Z||000000||qwikgta||06-08-2001||11:59 PM||||I just got a set of Beretta GTZ wheels for my 88 coupe.  They look great, but I have noticed a "rubbing" sound when I put on the brakes and once and a while when I take a sharp corner.  I know the fit is tight, but when I put the rims on the car I looked it over and thought I had enough room.  The wheel spins with no brake parts hitting anything.  Anyway, I believe that with a 5/16-1/2 inch plate I will have enough room and should have no rubbing.  I have looked into getting longer wheel studs and at the local parts stores they only have stock replacement.  When I looked over the parts book I could not find any "other" studs that looked like they would fit.  Many had the correct additional lenght but not the correct "knurl" diameter.  (.75 inch i think)<BR>*** so here is the question, has anyone added longer wheel studs to the rear of a fiero, any year they are all the same for this part.  If so were did you get the studs and what are the size.   Thanks<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 65K, Header no Cat, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, swaybar, open element air cleaner, hood vents.<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html<BR>http://vafiero.com||||reg||1
Z||000001||Master_Sushi||06-09-2001||01:33 AM||||What is it you think wheels are rubing? I have 16 inch wheels on my 88 coupe and dont have any problums. I also have an 84 coupe with the same (I Think) 16 inch beretta gtz wheels and 205-55 pep boys tires  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> and it dosent have any clearence problums.||||reg||1
Z||000002||qwikgta||06-09-2001||01:55 AM||||Not sure.  when i am driving i dont hear any noise.  When i apply brake presure i hear a noise, like dud..dud..dud..dud and it is fast at first and slows down as I slow down, that is why i thought brakes.  I am going to get it up on the lift today and check it out.  It could just be the ebrake cable or brake hose ect.. in the way.<P>Rob||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||06-09-2001||11:05 AM||||I have heard that Beretta wheels/tires  would "hit" on the back of a Fiero, too. Maybe the spring perch on the strut? Please let us know what you find. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||06-09-2001||07:30 PM||||check with a local race car shop. race cars have to use extra long studs to pass inspection. as for whats rubbing, look at the spindles, calipers to see if theres a mark. also look on the inside of wheel rim. If its making noise, its got to be making a mark somewhere.Look for a nice new shiny spot.I had to grind a tiny spot off my front A arms to clear the weights on my rims. It was just on the corners where the lip of the arm is folded over.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 06-09-2001).]||||reg||1
